== 2.9 Chapter 2 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [Drama is said to exist as two texts because],
  [it is written and then published],
  [it has a title and a body],
  [there is a script and there is a performance],
  [it has acts and scenes]
)

#mcq(2, [A tragedy is a play in which],
  [a person of standing is brought down by an action he takes],
  [the hero is innocent throughout],
  [everyone dies],
  [fate destroys a good man without cause]
)

#mcq(3, [The error of judgment or defect of character that sets a tragedy going is],
  [catharsis],
  [anagnorisis],
  [nemesis],
  [hamartia]
)

#mcq(4, [The overweening pride that makes a tragic error possible is],
  [hamartia],
  [peripeteia],
  [bathos],
  [hubris]
)

#mcq(5, [The reversal of the hero's fortune is called],
  [nemesis],
  [peripeteia],
  [exposition],
  [anagnorisis]
)

#mcq(6, [Oedipus discovers that the man he killed was his father. This moment is],
  [anagnorisis],
  [hamartia],
  [peripeteia only],
  [catharsis]
)

#mcq(7, [The purging of pity and fear experienced by the audience at the close of a tragedy is],
  [catharsis],
  [nemesis],
  [hubris],
  [denouement]
)

#mcq(8, [Classical tragedy differs from Shakespearean tragedy chiefly in that its downfall usually comes from],
  [the villain's plotting],
  [the hero's character],
  [forces outside the hero, such as fate or the gods],
  [the audience's expectations]
)

#mcq(9, [A comedy is best identified by],
  [a happy hero],
  [the absence of serious issues],
  [the presence of jokes],
  [an ending that enlarges the community]
)

#mcq(10, [A play in which two lovers are kept apart by a blocking figure until a marriage resolves the block is],
  [a comedy of humours],
  [romantic comedy],
  [a farce],
  [a comedy of manners]
)

#mcq(11, [A character ruled entirely by jealousy, to the point of caricature, belongs to],
  [melodrama],
  [romantic comedy],
  [tragicomedy],
  [comedy of humours]
)

#mcq(12, [A comedy whose laughter is directed at affectation and social behaviour in drawing rooms is],
  [comedy of manners],
  [farce],
  [morality play],
  [romantic comedy]
)

#mcq(13, [Satire differs from other comedy in that],
  [it is always written in verse],
  [it contains no laughter],
  [its ridicule has a corrective target],
  [it ends in death]
)

#mcq(14, [Farce depends chiefly on],
  [moral judgment],
  [poetic language],
  [character development],
  [improbable situation and speed]
)

#mcq(15, [A play in which virtue is persecuted by a wholly villainous figure and rescued at the last moment is],
  [tragicomedy],
  [farce],
  [melodrama],
  [satire]
)

#mcq(16, [Tragicomedy is distinguished from tragedy by],
  [its comic subplot],
  [its use of prose],
  [an ending in which the characters survive the threat],
  [the absence of danger]
)

#mcq(17, [A play that ends exactly where it began, with two characters still waiting, is best described as],
  [a history play],
  [an absurd play],
  [a comedy],
  [a morality play]
)

#mcq(18, [A morality play is one in which],
  [the play is read rather than staged],
  [abstractions appear as characters],
  [historical kings are dramatised],
  [the plot is taken from scripture]
)

#mcq(19, [A play written for performance on radio must],
  [include stage directions for lighting],
  [use many characters],
  [make every effect audible],
  [be written in verse]
)

#mcq(20, [A closet drama is a play],
  [performed in a small room],
  [written to be read rather than staged],
  [with a single character],
  [set entirely indoors]
)

#mcq(21, [The mark of the history play is that],
  [it is historically accurate],
  [public events carry the private plot],
  [it is always in blank verse],
  [it has no comic scenes]
)

#mcq(22, [Total theatre in African drama means that],
  [the audience sits all round the stage],
  [the cast is large],
  [the play is long],
  [song, dance, drum, mime and speech are the action itself]
)

#mcq(23, [A group of villagers who comment in song on the chief's conduct is functioning as],
  [comic relief],
  [a soliloquy],
  [a chorus],
  [an aside]
)

#mcq(24, [Audience participation in African drama typically takes the form of],
  [ticketed seating],
  [call and response, and direct address],
  [programme notes],
  [silent attention]
)

#mcq(25, [The communal protagonist in much African drama means that the crisis belongs to],
  [the leading actor],
  [the playwright],
  [the chorus alone],
  [a household, lineage or town]
)

#mcq(26, [Story differs from plot in that story is],
  [the events in the order the text gives them],
  [the theme of the work],
  [the events in the order they happened],
  [the setting of the action]
)

#mcq(27, [The stage of a plot in which the situation is displayed before it is disturbed is],
  [the exposition],
  [the falling action],
  [the denouement],
  [the climax]
)

#mcq(28, [The point at which the outcome of the action is decided is],
  [the exposition],
  [the denouement],
  [the complication],
  [the climax]
)

#mcq(29, [The untying of the knot at the close of a play is called],
  [the anagnorisis],
  [the peripeteia],
  [the denouement],
  [the exposition]
)

#mcq(30, [Aristotle's unity of action requires that],
  [the play happen in one place],
  [everything in the plot contribute to the one action],
  [the play last one day],
  [there be no subplot]
)

#mcq(31, [A subplot that repeats the main plot's mistake in a second household usually functions to],
  [fill stage time],
  [delay the climax],
  [comment on the main action by parallel],
  [provide comic relief only]
)

#mcq(32, [A stage direction reading *She does not move* most likely functions to],
  [indicate the set],
  [make a refusal visible in silence],
  [mark the interval],
  [tell the actor where to stand]
)

#mcq(33, [Which is NOT a feature of melodrama?],
  [simplified virtue],
  [characters capable of contradiction],
  [simplified villainy],
  [a last-minute rescue]
)

#mcq(34, [A play about a household in debt ends with the bond torn up. That moment is the],
  [exposition],
  [denouement],
  [falling action],
  [climax]
)

#mcq(35, [The most reliable test for telling tragedy from comedy is],
  [the number of deaths],
  [the rank of the characters],
  [the length of the play],
  [the direction of the ending]
)

#mcq(36, [A play in which the danger is genuine and the escape is earned is],
  [tragicomedy],
  [melodrama],
  [farce],
  [absurd drama]
)

#mcq(37, [The masque differs from other dramatic forms chiefly in that],
  [spectacle, music and dance carry it],
  [it is always comic],
  [it has no characters],
  [it is written in prose]
)

#mcq(38, [A question asking for “the function of the subplot” is best answered by pointing to],
  [the number of characters in it],
  [the act in which it begins],
  [its length],
  [its echo of the main plot's theme]
)

#mcq(39, [“The hero's ambition is his hamartia.” This statement is],
  [acceptable only in comedy],
  [acceptable if the ambition is the error that starts the fall],
  [wrong, because ambition is never a flaw],
  [wrong, because hamartia means fate]
)

#mcq(40, [A play whose hero is a king, whose downfall comes from his own decision, and whose last scene leaves the stage empty but the city intact is],
  [a history play],
  [a melodrama],
  [a farce],
  [a tragedy]
)

