  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The script is the words on the page; the performance is those words in bodies, light and time. Every question about stagecraft reasons from one to the other.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Standing, agency, irreversible consequence. Multiple deaths are common but not definitional, and total innocence removes the agency that makes the fall tragic.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hamartia is the initiating error. Nemesis is the retribution, catharsis the purging, anagnorisis the recognition.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hubris is excessive pride and a species of hamartia rather than a synonym for it. Peripeteia is reversal, bathos an anticlimax.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Peripeteia is the turn. Anagnorisis is the recognition that often accompanies it.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The discovery is the recognition. The change of fortune it produces is the reversal, and the two are distinct even when simultaneous.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Catharsis is the audience's emotional release. Denouement is the untying of the plot, which produces it.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Classical tragedy often works through fate or prophecy; Shakespearean tragedy locates the cause in the hero's own nature.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The direction of the ending is the test: a marriage, a reunion, a reconciliation. Jokes are a method, not a definition.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    That is the romantic-comedy engine. Comedy of humours runs on a ruling temperament, comedy of manners on wit and etiquette.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A single ruling temperament is the humour. The form laughs at the person who is only one thing.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The comedy of manners tests a social class by its speech and etiquette; wit is the action of the play.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Satire aims at vice or folly with an intention to correct, and its target may lie outside the play as well as inside it.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Doors, coincidences and mistaken identities. Farce does not criticise its characters; it overwhelms them.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Simplified virtue and villainy plus a last-minute rescue is melodrama. Tragicomedy keeps its characters capable of contradiction.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The threat in tragicomedy is genuine; the ending honours the possibility of survival. A comic subplot is not required.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Circular structure and unexplained waiting are marks of the absurd, and the absence of change is why it is not a comedy.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Allegorical figures such as Everyman and Fellowship are the defining mark. Scripture supplies the miracle play, chronicle the history play.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    With no set and no faces, sound carries everything: cues, silence and voice become the whole theatrical apparatus.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is composed for the reader, and its long speeches and slight stage business follow from that choice.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The nation becomes a character, and private lives are weighed against public consequence. Accuracy is not the criterion.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The elements are not decoration around the action; they carry it. That is the inheritance of a performance tradition.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A group outside the action that comments on it is a chorus, a staple of African drama as of Greek.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The audience is addressed and answers, so the boundary between stage and house is deliberately porous.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The unit in crisis is often a family or a community, which changes what counts as a resolution.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Story is chronological; plot is the arrangement, with its withholdings. The distance between them produces suspense.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Exposition establishes who wants what and what stands in the way, before the obstacle is engaged.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The climax is the highest tension and the moment of decision; the denouement is the untying that follows.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Denouement literally means untying. Peripeteia is the reversal and anagnorisis the recognition.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Unity of action is the one still enforced. Unity of time and place are the other two, and both are conventions rather than laws.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Doubling lets the audience see the same error punished in one case and forgiven in another, which is argument by parallel.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A negative direction is nearly always about visible resistance, and it carries meaning the dialogue does not state.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Contradiction is exactly what melodrama denies its characters; they are positions, not people.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The decision that settles the outcome is the climax. The consequences that follow belong to the falling action.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Tragedy closes in isolation or destruction, comedy in an enlarged community. Suffering may be present in either.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Real threat plus a survivable, earned ending is the tragicomic formula. Farce's dangers are contrivances.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The masque is courtly spectacle; the words serve the scenery and the music rather than the reverse.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Function questions want a relationship, and the subplot's relationship to the main plot is one of parallel or contrast.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ambition can be a hamartia when it is the error of judgment that initiates the fall. The term does not mean fate, and it is not confined to tragedy.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Standing, agency and an irreversible fall make it a tragedy. A history play would weigh the nation's fate, and the other two lack the hero's agency or gravity.
  ]

]

